## Adding a new blog post

1. Go to `blog_src/posts/`.
2. Copy an existing `.md` file as a starting point.
3. Rename it — the filename itself doesn't matter much, but starting it
   with the date keeps the folder sorted, e.g. `2026-10-03-some-title.md`.
4. Edit the front matter at the top:
   ```
   ---
   title: Your post title
   date: 2026-10-03
   ---
   ```
5. Write the body in Markdown underneath.
6. From the site's root folder, run:
   ```
   python3 blog_src/build_blog.py
   ```
   This regenerates every post page, the blog index, and the three
   "Latest Blog" teaser cards on the landing page — so you never
   hand-edit the sidebar or the teaser cards yourself.

Requires Python 3 with `markdown` and `pyyaml` (`pip install markdown pyyaml`).

## Contact

Instead of a contact form, the Contact section just shows your email
address, assembled at runtime by `js/email-obfuscate.js` rather than
written out as plain text in the page source. This isn't bulletproof
against every scraper, but it stops the simple ones that just
regex-match static HTML for "name@domain" patterns. With JavaScript
disabled, visitors see a readable but non-clickable fallback
("rebecca [dot] nicholson [at] northumbria [dot] ac [dot] uk").

**Double-check the address is right** — I used
`rebecca.nicholson@northumbria.ac.uk` based on the standard Northumbria
naming convention, but I haven't confirmed this is your actual address.
To change it, edit the two arrays near the top of
`js/email-obfuscate.js`:
```js
var user = ["rebecca", "nicholson"].join(".");
var domain = ["northumbria", "ac", "uk"].join(".");
```
and update the matching fallback text in `index.html`'s Contact section
(the `<h4 id="email-display">` element) to match.

